    <section>
        <h2>🔬 Research Background</h2>
        <p style="text-align: justify;">Learning Management Systems (LMS) have transformed higher education globally [1], but African universities continue to experience cycles of fragmented adoption and abandonment of Learning Management Systems. Historically, LMSs were introduced in African universities through donor-funded projects from the 1990s onward, with emphasis on technical infrastructure rather than pedagogical integration or institutional sustainability. This approach led to a disconnect. Platforms like Moodle or Blackboard were deployed, but without considering the realities of lecturer’s workloads, research-centered incentives, or infrastructural instability. While the COVID-19 pandemic drove a temporary surge in LMS usage, many institutions reverted to previous practices once the crisis waned, exposing deeper structural flaws beyond technical limitations [2].<br><br>
            These challenges are not unique to Africa but are characteristic of under-resourced global contexts. Issues such as funding instability, lecturer casualization, and the prioritization of research over teaching innovation are common in similar economies worldwide [3][4][5]. However, the diversity within Africa, from well-resourced institutions to low-resourced institutions with limited connectivity, demands solutions that reflect this wide spectrum of needs.
            <br><br>This study reframes the discourse from simply "LMS adoption" to the design of context-resilient online learning ecosystems. Rather than asking whether standardized platforms fit Africa, the research explores how physical-digital environments can evolve within institutional constraints, leveraging local strengths and realities..</p>
